  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The findings are based on three Mediterranean cities and may not generalize to different biomes or cultural contexts where human-bird relationships and urban development patterns differ.",
      "what": "This study combined 637 in-person surveys with bird censuses across three Mediterranean cities to analyze how bird-mediated cultural ecosystem services (CES) and ecosystem disservices (ED) vary between clumped and scattered urban developments. The researchers identified four key CES (identity, educational, inspirational, and bequest) and their associated bird species, finding that bequest services were higher in high-density clumped developments while disservices were higher in low-density scattered developments.",
      "why": "The research demonstrates that variations in urban nature experiences are driven primarily by differences in human perception and socio-demographic factors rather than actual changes in local bird species assembly. This shifts the focus of urban conservation from purely ecological management to managing human-wildlife relations and public perceptions."
    }
  },
